{"id": "d6d029c90d4cddf1:tutorial:gpt-6-luna:", "source_id": "d6d029c90d4cddf1", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/save-and-invest/save-rainy-day", "topic": "Save for a Rainy Day", "subtopic": null, "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "there’s a tradeoff between security and availability; your money earns a low interest rate.", "question": null, "answer": "Savings accounts and similar places can keep money accessible and relatively secure, which can be useful for emergencies. The tradeoff is that they usually earn a low interest rate. If that interest does not keep up with inflation, your savings may buy less over time. This is why people may keep some money in savings while considering investing for longer-term growth.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 940, "output_tokens": 186}}
{"id": "d6d029c90d4cddf1:qa:gpt-6-luna:", "source_id": "d6d029c90d4cddf1", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/save-and-invest/save-rainy-day", "topic": "Save for a Rainy Day", "subtopic": null, "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Most smart investors put enough money in savings to cover an emergency, like sudden unemployment.", "question": "Why do people often keep some money in savings for emergencies?", "answer": "Savings can provide money that is available when an emergency arises, such as sudden unemployment.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 940, "output_tokens": 158}}
{"id": "d6d029c90d4cddf1:misconception:gpt-6-luna:", "source_id": "d6d029c90d4cddf1", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/save-and-invest/save-rainy-day", "topic": "Save for a Rainy Day", "subtopic": null, "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "That is why many people put some of their money in savings, but look to investing so they can earn more over longer periods of time.", "question": "Misconception: Money in a savings account will always keep the same purchasing power over time.", "answer": "A savings account may offer security and access to your money, but its interest may not keep up with inflation. As a result, the money could buy less in the future than it does today.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 940, "output_tokens": 207}}
